\paragraph{Contributions and findings.}
(i) A family-held-out, leakage-free, paired protocol for the which-$k$ question, with a closure measure of how much of the gap between the column-mean and the leave-one-benchmark-out predictor is closed, and the smallest $k^*$ that closes a given share. (ii) On real matrices a few benchmarks capture most of the information: three close 81--96\% of the gap, but 90\% needs one benchmark on HELM MMLU and nine or more on MTEB, with wide intervals, so $k^*$ is reported with its interval. (iii) Greedy A-optimal selection beats random selection by 6--11\% of the error averaged over $k=1,\dots,10$, mostly at $k\le3$, and leverage scores and pivoted QR do worse than random on MTEB; on MTEB matrices with 21--70\% of cells observed, greedy selection also beats random and closes 0.71--0.80 of the gap at $k=3$ (pairwise-correlation predictor). (iv) New families are predicted about as well as seen ones on MTEB but 14\% worse on HELM MMLU, models released later are predicted 9--18\% worse at $k=3$ on the same test models, and the strongest models are predicted too low. (v) A closed-form risk is exact with known parameters and gives the shape of the error-versus-$k$ curve, but on real data it is 36--69\% off in level, and a coherence argument for when random selection is near-greedy does not explain our data. Code and data are released as described in Appendix~\ref{app:repro}.
