\paragraph{Pre-specified criteria.}
Four hypotheses and their decision rules were fixed in the study plan before the final runs; the departures from the plan are listed in the released code (Appendix~\ref{app:repro}). H1: $k^*(0.9)$ lies within $[r_{\rm eff},2r_{\rm eff}]$ ``knee near $r_{\rm eff}$'' on most pools, where $r_{\rm eff}$ is the smallest rank within one standard error of the best held-out likelihood; the alternatives are ``dominated by a general factor'' ($k^*<r_{\rm eff}$) and ``above window'' ($k^*>2r_{\rm eff}$ or censored). H2: greedy A-optimal selection beats random selection, with a grid-averaged paired gap in RMSE over $k=1,\dots,\min(10,N/2)$ whose family-bootstrap interval excludes zero and which is at least 10\% of the random error. H3: random-split accuracy overstates accuracy for new families and newer models. H4: the closed-form reducible risk of Section~\ref{sec:theory} is within 15\% (10\% for synthetic data with fitted parameters) of the empirical one. The headline $k^*$ is the smaller of the $k^*$ of the factor model and of the best simple predictor (chosen per fold on training rows), because $k^*$ belongs to the selector and predictor together ; we show both and their intervals, since a minimum of two noisy estimates is biased low.

\subsection{Which benchmarks to run?}
Greedy A-optimal selection is better than random selection on all three pools (Figure~\ref{fig:gap}), by a grid-averaged paired gap in RMSE of $-0.038$ [$-0.058,-0.013$] on MTEB-Eng, $-0.076$ [$-0.085,-0.066$] on MTEB-Multi and $-0.044$ [$-0.065,-0.026$] on HELM MMLU, i.e. 6.1, 11.3 and 7.6\% of the random error. Only MTEB-Multi meets the pre-specified margin of 10\%, so H2 holds there and the others are a clear but small improvement. The benefit is concentrated at small $k$: 5--18\% of the random error at $k\le3$ (the interval includes zero on MTEB-Eng at $k=3$), 1--5\% at $k=10$, below 3\% at $k=20$. Greedy D-optimality and $k$-medoids on benchmark correlations are as good as greedy A; maximum-variance selection is no better than random, and leverage scores and pivoted QR are \emph{worse} than random on the MTEB pools (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:grid}). Adaptive selection gains at most 0.014 RMSE over greedy A (MTEB-Multi only, Appendix Table~\ref{tab:adaptive}). The factor model is no more accurate than simple predictors on the same sets: ridge, $k$-NN and Ledoit--Wolf conditioning have equal or lower RMSE on the MTEB pools (by up to 0.026) and ridge and Ledoit--Wolf are 0.011--0.013 lower on HELM MMLU (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:predictors}), so the factor model's value lies in the selector and the risk formula, not in the point prediction. The MTEB-Eng gap depends on the transform: with the transform not chosen on the training folds (logit) it is $-0.017$ [$-0.051,0.037$] (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:robust}).

